WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.580
Okay, little VidCon update: I officially have a room,
time, and date for my workshop.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:16.420
My workshop will be on Friday, July 24th,
from 3:30pm to 4:30pm at Room 205.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.900
And the workshop is called Lights, Camera,
Caption.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.440
Credit to my boyfriend for thinking of that.
[clap]

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.320
If you were planning on coming to my closed
captioning workshop, that’s all the info

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:32.480
that you need to know. Write it down and if
you haven’t registered for VidCon yet, go

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.420
do that.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.340
Now that that is settled, I am now doing little
videos to help prep me for what I want to

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.680
talk about in my workshop.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:42.740
Starting with this one.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:48.380
So as you know, and if you haven’t known,
I’ve been e-mailing, tweeting, and sometimes

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:55.039
handwriting letters to a plethora of YouTubers
telling them and asking them that they need

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:56.479
to closed caption their videos.

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:02.699
Sometimes, I’ll leave a comment under their
video telling them or asking them to closed

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:03.779
caption their videos.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.300
Like when Ashley Mardell announced her little
project, I asked her to closed caption her

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:09.680
video.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.880
When Veronica made a video about skincare,
I asked her to closed caption her video because

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:21.700
these are important videos that have information.
Not just comedic videos.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.040
And sometimes after I leave a comment under
a video, I get a comment from either the creator

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:35.560
themselves or the viewer, er, a viewer, telling
me that I should, or that there should be

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:42.480
a CC button between this and that on the video
and that should give me the captions.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:46.920
As if I haven't tried that before already
before I left a comment.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.920
but I don't get fussy with it, I just say
something like,

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.320
"Well, you know, I'm a Deaf vlogger that advocates
for better closed captioning or some closed

00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:02.300
captioning, at all, on YouTube and there's
a big possibility I've already checked to

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.540
see if there's closed captioning before I
left this comment.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:11.100
And even if I wasn't a vlogger, there's still
a chance I checked for the captioning before

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.090
I left this comment.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.170
There's no closed captioning on this video,
I promise.”

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.600
And this is actually something that happened
recently, and the person replied with,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.900
"I'm just trying to help, I thought this would
be better than nothing"

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.600
When somebody leaves me, or anybody else this
comment, and it's happened before from even

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:31.980
bigger YouTubers,

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:42.700
I think, if I try to help somebody I do try
to make sure and check my possible answers

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.420
a little bit before I actually insert my answer
and give it to them.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.530
So, I check to make sure that I'm a little
bit right before I give it and if I'm not

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.720
sure, then I'll leave a note that says, "I'm
not 100% sure, so you know, maybe look it

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:57.740
up yourself as well."

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:04.080
So, I know that when so and so tells me, or
anybody else to click that button and it'll

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:11.280
just automatically give it to me, I know that
this person has not actually looked at the

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.660
stuff themselves.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:19.540
And that’s when I say, “Automatic cRaptions
are not captions.”

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:26.100
Automatic closed "cRaptioning" is not closed
captioning, and that's when I just ask politely,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.260
and I ask,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.140
"Have you actually looked at these things
yourself?"

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:38.180
Because, if you had, you would know that it's
completely nonsensical and it won’t help

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:38.709
me at all.

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:40.909
"Well, I just thought it was better than nothing."

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:47.960
“Okay, but answer my question. Have you
actually tried it, yourself? Have you muted

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.550
the video? Just mute the video.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:56.960
Read the automatic "cRaptions" and let me
know if you understand what this video’s

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:57.460
about.”

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.020
And then I never got a reply. In fact, I think
the person actually deleted the entire comment

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:08.550
thread which is a little disappointing because
I'm not trying to argue. I just want to - instead

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.040
of me just saying,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.160
"Those aren't captions."

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.040
I am trying to get people to look at them.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:22.960
Pretend you’re me, mute the video or lower
the volume significantly, and look at it and

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.190
you will see, you will have an actual experience
that tells you -

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.730
That’s not working.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:35.330
Now, sometimes people will take screenshots
of the automatic cRaptions and post about

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.940
it and then they laugh at it and laugh at
how ridiculous it all sounds.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.980
Sometimes, automatic cRaptions are really
funny. I’ll give that. They’re funny.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:48.650
There’s nothing wrong with laughing at ridiculous
captions.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:57.540
But instead of just giggling at the ridiculousness
of terrible automatic cRaptions, I’m thinking,

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:03.530
“Why don’t we just think about this a
little bit more and think about those who

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:09.870
need actual closed captioning and just think
about how this does not help.”

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:16.640
And just like I’m going to do in my workshop,
I’m going to show you some examples of automatic

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:17.330
cRaptions.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:25.300
And I’m going to give you videos, or examples
from videos of people that are going to VidCon.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.040
Featured creators just like me.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:34.780
And that means I can show you almost every
single creator - at least one video per creator

00:05:34.789 --> 00:05:36.749
- but then that would just make this way too
long.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:43.960
Let’s start with the oh-so-popular Grace
[Helbig] who I think is a very nice person

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:48.000
and can be hilarious; however, her stuff is
not captioned.

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:51.919
Here’s an example from her “What’s Up
My Butt Challenge”.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.620
“well I I’m here and I remembered me out
I am today”

00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:00.949
“where playing a game that is based on a
challenge that exist on you to call but”

00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:02.249
“what’s in my mouth challenge”

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:09.020
Now I know she’s done a What’s In My Mouth
Challenge video, I’m pretty sure, but if

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:14.620
somebody has not watched that video or doesn’t
know who Grace is, and “What’s Up My Butt

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:20.240
Challenge” was the first video somebody
saw, this makes no sense whatsoever.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.140
And Grace not captioning her videos always
kind of makes me giggle. I find it a little

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:25.930
bit ironic -

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:30.290
Is that how you use this word in this situation?
Let me continue.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:35.919
On page 71 in her book “The Art of Pretending
to Be A Grown Up,” and I’ve reviewed this

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:37.599
and I’ve mentioned it in that video -

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:46.500
When she was younger, she did a few internships
at MTV transcribing videos for My Super Sweet

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:47.010
16.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:55.920
So she transcribed for an MTV show, why don’t
you - or find somebody to transcribe your

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:56.440
videos?

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:02.449
And when you look at the automatic cRaptions
on her video…

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:12.139
There’s a Tumblr that actually transcribes
every single video and the Tumblr person and

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:19.639
I have been trying to contact Grace on Twitter,
tried to find an e-mail just, you know, offering

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:24.059
to send these transcripts so all Grace needs
to do is upload them.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:27.700
The work’s already done. She just needs
to upload them because we don’t know the

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:28.930
login to her account.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.240
So that’s gonna take some work.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:38.440
And then we have Smosh who is a duo on YouTube
that has 20 million subscribers. That’s

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:39.580
all I know.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:44.800
Btut Caption Action 2 has been trying to get
me to do a video, a separate video, on Smosh

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:52.229
because Smosh is premiering a movie at VidCon
the day before my workshop, unfortunately,

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:57.589
and.. 20 million subscribers and you don’t
caption your videos.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.620
So from “The Murder Party”

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:05.419
“woman so the mail on the mailman okay male
woman just sounds weird hear anyone”

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:07.399
[MOUTHS: WHAT THE FUCK?]

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:09.660
&gt;From “Worst Sex Ever: Behind the Scenes”

00:08:09.669 --> 00:08:13.969
“i think is in doubt about a future together
because the fans are fickle ass”

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.100
1 - that’s not how you compliment your fans.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:21.740
2 - I don’t know what that’s actually
trying to say. I don’t know what the audio

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:28.160
says, but “because the fans are fickle ass”
does not sound like something I would want

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.700
to say or want my video to say.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:36.969
So while I don’t know if I can get Smosh
to caption their movie before VidCon, uhm…

00:08:36.969 --> 00:08:38.339
Hopefully, it -

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.260
Hopefully, with some work, I can get Smosh
to caption their videos eventually.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.660
And then we have AmazingPhil “I Destroyed
My House”

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:49.919
“one second caveman for dinosaurs you can
make a bet”

00:08:50.009 --> 00:08:55.209
That’s like the very first line in that
video and, uhm, I - I don’t understand.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:01.360
And then I decided to mention this guy because
he has mentioned almost every time he does

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:07.610
a YouNow that the closed captioning on YouNow
- or, I’m sorry - the automatic closed cRaptioning

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:11.420
- on YouNow is completely nonsensical and
it’s awful

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:17.200
But this guy does not closed caption his videos
at all and that is Nathan aka TheThirdPew.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.500
I am a big fan of Nathan.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:23.939
I’ve been a fan of Nathan since the
Sam Pepper callout video.

00:09:23.939 --> 00:09:29.410
And I think a lot of people would enjoy his
channel, but it’s not accessible.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:30.550
Here’s an example.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.160
With his newest video “Do YouTubers Have
Responsibility?”

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:40.040
“follow-up as long as a crazy today girl
is going be quite heated acre lot eyes even”

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:46.480
Nathan, I know you look at the automatic closed
cRaptioning on your YouNow. Can you please

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:48.120
look at it on your own videos?

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:54.780
This is why it’s so important for YouTubers
to caption their videos and while it can be

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:58.730
a tedious process depending on how long your
videos are -

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:02.890
I know. I did a 22 minute video that took
four days.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:09.640
But if I, the Deaf vlogger, can do it, hearing
people, you can do it.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:13.480
And if you don’t have times, there’s many,
many options.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.880
If you’ve got the money, you can pay to
have them done for a very affordable price.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.700
You can get volunteers on Amara to do it.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:28.020
There’s a thing on some YouTube channels
that lets people contribute closed captioning.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:33.480
Or you can just simply ask people to e-mail
transcripts which is what I’m doing for

00:10:33.489 --> 00:10:40.699
people who are subtitling my videos into Spanish
or Portuguese or any other language that is

00:10:40.699 --> 00:10:41.319
not English.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:48.899
I mean, this time, I’m showing you the proof.
I’m showing you examples of this nonsensical

00:10:48.899 --> 00:10:51.399
automatic closed cRaptioning.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:11:00.380
If you mute these videos and then you watch
them with those on, you can’t understand

00:11:00.389 --> 00:11:00.909
the videos.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.540
Automatic cRaptions are not better than nothing.
They’re really not and that is proof.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:13.819
Yes, it may get some words right. It may get
an entire sentence right, but when 95% of

00:11:13.819 --> 00:11:19.219
the video makes no sense, they’re not better
than nothing.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:25.180
So my plan now is, of course, to keep working
on my workshop and to do the workshop.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:32.180
I’ve given you the info of when, where it
is. Friday, July 24th. 3:30-4:30pm at Room

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:33.060
205.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:36.300
So come see me and…

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:42.240
Not only that, but I’m going to keep trying
to e-mail, tweet, uh I think I’m going to

00:11:42.249 --> 00:11:46.809
try to handwrite a letter to every single
featured creator - even if I’ve already

00:11:46.809 --> 00:11:47.369
done it -

00:11:47.369 --> 00:11:53.629
It’s easy to delete an e-mail. It’s also
easy to throw away a letter, but you do have

00:11:53.629 --> 00:11:58.689
to open up a handwritten letter and look at
it, so it’s a little harder to miss than

00:11:58.689 --> 00:11:59.189
an e-mail.

00:11:59.189 --> 00:12:04.600
Like I said in other videos related to my
workshop, if you’ve got ideas on what I

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:08.580
can talk about in my workshop, feel free to
leave ‘em in the comments.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.320
I have a loose outline right now.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:12.500
I’ve just got work to do.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:15.720
So yeah, I will see you in my next video.
Bye.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:20.000
Subtitles by the Amara.org community

